DA-004 — Scene 07 — Control Test — Approved Draft v1.1

Neither of them moved.

The camera sat charged on the dresser. Its screen had gone dark from inactivity, but the green light at the charger remained steady. The recorder lay beside it where Eli had left it before going downstairs. Nothing had recorded the three impacts they had just heard.

Martin looked toward the bathroom, then toward the wall where the first knock had come from.

“We’re done.”

Eli kept his eyes on him.

“I mean it,” Martin said. “Turn it off. Get some sleep.”

“It isn’t on.”

“You know what I mean.”

Martin crossed to the dresser and unplugged the camera. He wound the cord once around his hand, too tightly at first, then loosened it and set it beside the charger. Eli watched him do it. Twenty minutes earlier Martin had been tearing through the floor trying to find a door, a pipe, a vent, anything that could make the sounds. Now his movements had the opposite purpose. Camera away. Cord away. Investigation over.

Eli looked at the room.

The two chairs were still displaced, but there was nothing left in them to investigate. Martin had moved them. The fact had survived the argument exactly as it had started: ordinary.

The knocks had not.

“If I leave,” Eli said, “and it happens again—”

Martin turned. “No.”

“Listen.”

“No.”

“If I leave you here and walk the hall, then there are three possibilities.”

“There are about three hundred possibilities.”

“I’m not saying it proves anything.”

“That usually comes right before you tell me what something proves.”

Eli let that pass.

“If you hear it in here while I’m gone, that matters. If I hear it out there and you don’t, that matters. If neither of us hears it, that matters too.”

Martin rubbed his eyes with thumb and forefinger.

“You’re still in the same building.”

“I know.”

“There are still people in the building.”

“I know.”

“Pipes. Doors. Elevators. Somebody dropping a shoe two rooms over.”

“I know.”

“And if you hear three noises in the hallway, you don’t get to call them the same three noises because you want them to be.”

“I know.”

Martin lowered his hand.

Four times he had answered I know without pushing back. He kept his eyes on Martin and left the possibilities separate.

Martin glanced at the door.

“One walk,” Eli said. “I stay on this floor. I don’t go behind anything locked. I don’t ask it to knock. You stay here. We see what happens.”

“We see what happens,” Martin repeated. “That’s all.”

“That’s all.”

“And if nothing happens?”

“I come back.”

Martin looked at him for several seconds.

Martin stepped away from the dresser. Eli took the opening.

He took the camera, checked the battery, and turned it on. The night-vision image opened in a grainy wash over the room. He filmed the time on his phone, then Martin standing near the bed.

“Dad stays in the room,” he said to the camera. “I’m walking the guest hallway. We’re not reproducing the knocks. We’re not prompting for them.”

Martin’s mouth tightened slightly at the camera-facing voice, but he said nothing.

Eli opened his equipment pouch for the handheld light and saw the lollipop.

He had forgotten about it.

The guide had given it to them on the tour hours earlier, along with the story about children in the upper corridors and guests leaving candy for them. Eli had put it away because he had planned to try it after the room session.

He held the pouch open for a second before taking the candy.

He picked it up anyway.

Martin noticed.

“Seriously?”

“I was already going to use it.”

“For the kids.”

“For the story about the kids.”

“That sounds different to you?”

“Tonight it does.”

Eli slid the wrapped candy into his jacket pocket.

Martin stared at him.

“It’s not the test,” Eli said. “I’m taking it because I already brought it.”

“Good distinction.”

“You want me to leave it?”

“I want you to go to bed.”

Eli opened the door.

Martin did not try to stop him.

The corridor outside looked smaller through the camera than it had during the source hunt. The lens flattened the distance between the repeating doors and compressed the bends in the hall until the far end seemed almost close enough to touch. In person, Eli could still feel the length of it in his legs.

He turned back once.

Martin stood inside the room with one hand on the door edge.

“One walk,” he said.

Eli nodded.

Martin let go.

Eli pulled the door nearly shut, leaving Martin alone on the other side.

For the first minute, Eli recorded plenty and heard nothing resembling the pattern.

Ventilation moved steadily through the ceiling grilles. Somewhere below him, water ran through a wall in a long descending rush. An elevator motor started, carried vibration into the floor, then stopped. A few seconds later came the soft mechanical clunk of doors opening somewhere he could not see.

Eli walked past the first guest-room door and listened.

Nothing.

The second.

Nothing.

At the third he heard a television, low enough that he could not make out words.

He moved on.

The camera clock advanced.

He passed the stretch where he and Martin had tested the transmitted door sounds earlier. He stopped there and listened long enough for his breathing to become the loudest thing on the camera microphone.

A pipe clicked.

Then nothing.

He waited.

No second impact followed. No third.

Eli continued.

He kept the camera pointed ahead instead of at himself. When he spoke, he kept his voice low.

“Still on the fourth floor. Dad’s still in the room. No knocks.”

He almost added no activity and stopped himself.

There had been activity. The elevator. Plumbing. A television. Air through vents. What he meant was that none of it had matched what he was testing.

At the next bend he took the lollipop from his pocket.

The wrapper made a dry crackle that sounded enormous in the empty hall.

Eli held it by the stem in front of the lens.

“This is the candy the guide gave us earlier.”

He paused.

The old version of the test came back to him automatically. Ask the children to move it. Ask them to come play. Repeat what the guide had told him and wait for something to fit.

He looked down the corridor.

Instead he said, “I’m holding it still.”

That was all.

He waited.

The candy did nothing.

Thirty seconds passed.

Then another thirty.

His shoulder began to ache from holding the camera chest-high. He shifted his grip. Somewhere far off, a guest-room latch clicked and a door opened. Eli heard several footsteps, then the elevator call button chimed.

Human movement.

He filmed the empty stretch ahead until the elevator carried whoever it was away.

The lollipop still hung from his other hand.

Eli walked on.

At the junction where the guest corridor bent toward the side of the floor connected to the service route, his stomach tightened.

He stopped.

The sensation came quickly enough that for a moment he thought he had stood up too fast, though he had been walking steadily for several minutes. Heat gathered behind his jaw. His mouth filled with saliva.

He lowered the camera.

“Okay.”

He leaned one shoulder against the wall and breathed through his nose.

The nausea strengthened, then held.

He turned the camera toward his face only long enough to document it.

“Just got nauseous here. At this junction.”

He swallowed.

“I don’t know why.”

No colder here that he could tell. No visible change in the light. No sound apart from the ventilation and a low mechanical vibration he could feel more easily through his shoulder against the wall than through his shoes.

He moved away from the wall.

The nausea did not vanish.

He walked several paces back the way he had come.

Waited.

It eased a little.

He returned to the junction.

His stomach turned again, though not as sharply.

Eli looked at the camera screen.

“That could be me expecting it now.”

He stopped recording commentary and simply left the camera running.

After another minute the feeling weakened enough that he could stand normally.

The lollipop was still in his left hand.

He had almost forgotten it.

He lifted it again, pinching the stem between thumb and forefinger. The wrapper reflected the infrared light in a pale flare.

Nothing happened.

He lowered his hand.

The stem slipped sideways between his fingers.

Eli closed his hand around it before it fell.

He froze.

For several seconds he stared at his own hand.

The stem had shifted between his fingers. That much had happened. He did not know whether the wrapper had caught his sleeve, whether his grip had loosened when he lowered his arm, whether a current of air had touched the broad plastic around the candy, or whether he had moved without noticing.

He repositioned his hand in front of the camera.

“That almost came out of my fingers.”

He waited.

Nothing.

He held it the same way again.

Nothing.

He moved his wrist down deliberately.

The wrapper brushed the cuff of his jacket.

He tried it twice more and could not make the candy slip in quite the same way.

Eli put it back in his pocket.

He did not ask anyone to do it again.

By the time he reached the far stretch of guest corridor, he had more ordinary noise on the recording than anything he could use.

He had separated himself from Martin. He had not separated either of them from the conditions Martin had warned him about.

Water moved somewhere behind the wall. A door shut at the far end. The elevator motor started again. Any three impacts he heard out here would still have sources he could not see.

He checked the camera clock.

More than twenty minutes.

No private cadence.

He walked back toward the junction at half his earlier pace.

He stopped prompting entirely.

He heard a fan somewhere behind a wall, a faint rush that could have been water, the small expansion clicks of old material changing temperature. Once, a dull impact came from below him.

Only one.

Eli waited for the rest.

Nothing followed.

He continued.

Five minutes later he sat on the edge of a narrow bench near the turn in the corridor and rested the camera against his knee. The nausea had dwindled to a faint uneasiness in his stomach. His hand had stopped shaking enough that he could read the remaining battery time without the numbers blurring.

The lollipop stayed in his pocket.

“No pattern,” he said quietly.

He considered saying the test had failed.

Martin could be hearing the rhythm in the room right now. Or nothing could be happening there either. Eli would not know until he went back.

He raised the camera.

“I’m going back in a minute.”

He stopped talking.

At first he thought the sound was water.

It came from somewhere beyond the bend to his left, faint enough that he could hear it only in the gaps between the ventilation cycles. A rising sound, then another beneath it.

Eli lifted his head.

The sound stopped.

He stood without moving his feet.

Nothing.

He brought the camera up and pointed it toward the bend.

Twenty seconds passed.

Then something sounded again.

Not a knock.

Not plumbing, at least not in the way the plumbing had sounded anywhere else that night.

A thin human register reached him from farther down the corridor—more than one tone overlapping, or one voice changing pitch. Too distant for words. It might have been talking. It might have been crying.

He remembered what the guide had told them about children in the upper corridors.

The word came immediately.

Children.

Eli did not say it.

He moved toward the bend.

The camera stayed aimed ahead.

“Human-sounding,” he whispered. “Farther down.”

He listened.

Nothing repeated.

He reached the first door and stopped beside it, keeping his body clear of the threshold. No television. No conversation. No movement he could hear through the wood.

The next door was equally quiet.

He continued.

The sound had seemed to come from the side of the floor that led back toward the service route they had walked earlier, but the corridor gave him no clean line to it. Guest doors interrupted one wall. The other turned around a structural section before narrowing toward the junction they had examined with Martin. Somewhere beyond that were staff spaces he could not enter.

Eli stopped at each door long enough to listen.

Nothing.

At the final bend, he heard the sound once more.

Brief.

Faint.

A wavering human noise from somewhere ahead.

He raised the camera but did not speak.

No name.

No age.

No story.

He followed it toward the service-passage side of the floor.

